  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1150 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nOur late week weather sets sail with absolutely pristine conditions, \nthanks to ridging aloft settling over the south Plains Thursday - \nFriday. Temperatures should stay in the 60s and 70s with light winds \nand no rain chances. While we'll avoid the gales of November, breezy \nsouth winds look to return Saturday when an approaching trough lifts \nnorth and leaves a tighter pressure gradient over the region \n(sustained 15-20 mph gusting to 35 mph). \n\nAs a Great Lakes low lifts to the NE CONUS, models show a secondary \ndeepening wave to our west sending increased southwest flow crashing \nover the Panhandles through the weekend. This pattern should \nincrease our moisture, which will ship improved H700 theta-e \nnorthward atop the region (PWATs as high as 0.75-1.0\"). Lift from \nthe system will support potential shower development for at least \nsouth and east portions of the Panhandles (20-50% POPs), but recent \ndeterministic and ensemble data trends are slowly weighing towards \nmore aggressive outcomes. The ECMWF and now even moreso the GFS show \nthe gloomy skies of November settling in next week as the secondary \nsystem sinks south Sunday night into Monday, which could provide yet \nanother round of rain chances. \n\nLong range projections aim to further wreck our stretch of nice \nweather, sending additional strong systems towards the Plains. \nConfidence is still quite low, but if these signals can come to \nfruition, continued rain, surges of cold air, and potentially even \nsome winter weather could be possible.\n\nHarrel",
